  SECURITY REPORT — Score: 71/100 [MEDIUM]
  The codebase has several high‑severity issues: overly permissive CORS, unauthenticated seed and stats endpoints, and an IDOR vulnerability in the user lookup route. Medium‑severity concerns include missing input length validation and a mass‑assignment risk in the user creation schema. Adding authentication, tightening CORS, validating ownership, enforcing field length limits, and implementing rate limiting will markedly improve security.

  Findings (7):
  [HIGH    ] CORSIssue in app/main.py
             CORS is configured to allow any origin ("*") which exposes the API to CSRF attacks and data leakage in production.
             Fix: Restrict `allow_origins` to a whitelist of trusted frontend domains, e.g., `allow_origins=["https://myapp.com"]`.
  [HIGH    ] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             The `/seed` endpoint is publicly accessible and can be invoked by anyone to create or modify database records.
             Fix: Protect the endpoint with authentication (e.g., `Depends(get_current_user)`) and restrict it to admin users only.
  [HIGH    ] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/stats_routes.py
             The statistics summary endpoint does not require authentication, allowing unauthenticated users to enumerate user and task counts.
             Fix: Add a dependency on `get_current_user` (or an admin‑only dependency) to require authentication.
  [HIGH    ] IDOR in app/routes/user_routes.py
             Any authenticated user can retrieve any other user's data by guessing IDs, because the endpoint does not verify ownership or role.
             Fix: Validate that `user_id == current_user.id` or that the current user has an admin role before returning the user record.
  [MEDIUM  ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/user.py
             String fields lack `max_length` constraints, allowing excessively long inputs that could cause DoS or buffer‑overflow style issues.
             Fix: Add `max_length` to all string fields, e.g., `Field(min_length=1, max_length=150)` for usernames.
  [MEDIUM  ] MassAssignment in app/schemas/user.py
             The `UserCreate` schema exposes a server‑controlled field (`hashed_password`) that could be set by a client, bypassing password hashing.
             Fix: Remove `hashed_password` from the input schema and only accept a plain `password` field; hash it server‑side.
  [LOW     ] RateLimiting in app/routes/auth_routes.py
             Login and registration endpoints have no rate‑limiting, making them vulnerable to credential‑stuffing and brute‑force attacks.
             Fix: Integrate a rate‑limiting solution (e.g., `slowapi` or a reverse‑proxy) to limit attempts per IP.

  Passed: No SQL injection found, Passwords properly hashed with bcrypt
